The NT loader hands argv[0] over as a full path -- drive letter,
backslashes and a .exe suffix -- and gnulib's program_name is what prefixes
every diagnostic diff, cmp, diff3 and sdiff print, both through error() and
through the eleven places those four mains read program_name DIRECTLY
("Usage: %s", "Try '%s --help'", "%s: diff failed: ").  Left alone, an
unrecognised option reads

    Z:\nix\store\...-diffutils-3.8\bin\diff.exe: unrecognized option: --bogus

which in a bootstrap, where a diagnostic is the only debugging channel there
is, is worse than useless: the store path is longer than the message.  It
was measured that way before this patch was written, not assumed.

gnulib strips a directory here ON PURPOSE, and its own comment says why -- a
user who runs /some/hidden/place/bin/cp wants to be told which cp it was.
That reasoning is sound on a system where argv[0] is what the user typed.
It is not this system: here argv[0] is always the loader's fully-qualified
NT path whatever the user typed, so the information gnulib is preserving is
never the information the argument was chosen for.  This is not the same
question as glibc's own error() printing program_invocation_name in full --
that only happens when the user themselves ran /usr/bin/diff by its full
path, which on this target is not a choice the user gets to make.

POSIX basename() cannot do this -- it splits on '/' only -- which is the
same reason tar, gzip, both gawks and findutils on this side carry a patch
of their own rather than calling it.  This is the same patch as findutils'
nt-program-name.patch, against the same gnulib file -- diffutils 3.8 ships
its own un-substituted copy of lib/progname.c rather than a .in.h template,
so the line numbers differ but the function does not.  Putting it in
set_program_name, once, is what makes one patch serve diff, cmp, diff3 and
sdiff and every one of the eleven direct reads of program_name alongside
error()'s: see shim/nt-missing.c's getprogname, which forwards whatever
program_name already is rather than trimming a second time.

The `.exe' suffix is deliberately LEFT ON, as it is for tar, gzip and gawk:
the file really is called diff.exe, and a diagnostic that names a file which
does not exist would be a second small lie in place of the large one.

--- lib/progname.c
+++ lib/progname.c
@@ -81,7 +81,19 @@
          cp: `foo' and `foo' are the same file
    */

-  program_name = argv0;
+  /* ... except on this target, where argv[0] is not what the user typed:
+     the NT loader always hands over its own fully-qualified path.  See
+     patches/nt-program-name.patch for why that makes gnulib's reasoning
+     above inapplicable here.  ':' is in the set for the drive letter, since
+     a path may have no separator after it.  */
+  {
+    const char *cursor;
+
+    program_name = argv0;
+    for (cursor = argv0; *cursor; cursor++)
+      if (*cursor == '/' || *cursor == '\\' || *cursor == ':')
+        program_name = cursor + 1;
+  }

